# ============================ PAYROLL 1 ============================
LESSONS_P1 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The pay pipeline in ZhiftERP", "11", "<p>This track certifies the payroll operator in ZhiftERP. This module covers the machinery that computes pay: the Employee record's payroll fields, Salary Components, the Salary Structure, the Salary Structure Assignment, formulas and payment days, and the preview procedure that verifies a structure before it pays anyone.</p><p><b>The pipeline.</b> Pay in ZhiftERP is computed by a chain of records. Learn the chain in order, because every module of this track works somewhere on it:</p><p>1. <b>Employee</b> — the person, with the payroll fields Chapter 2 walks.<br>2. <b>Salary Component</b> — one pay element: Basic, Housing, PAYE, Pension. Each component carries its type, its calculation, and its ledger account (Chapter 3).<br>3. <b>Salary Structure</b> — a named template listing which components make up a pay package (Chapter 4).<br>4. <b>Salary Structure Assignment</b> — the record that attaches a structure to one employee from a date, with that employee's base amount (Chapter 5).<br>5. <b>Payroll Entry</b> — the monthly run: selects the employees, generates their slips (module 3).<br>6. <b>Salary Slip</b> — one employee's computed pay for one period: earnings, deductions, net (modules 3-4).<br>7. <b>The posting and the payment</b> — the accrual Journal Entry the run books, and the bank payment (modules 2-3).</p><p><b>The design consequence.</b> Because pay is computed by this chain, the operator's work is maintaining <b>records</b>, not calculating amounts: when the structure is right, every slip is right; when a slip is wrong, the fault is in a record — a component's formula, an assignment's base, an employee's field — and the fix is made there, once, for every future month. A payroll kept in a spreadsheet beside the system has none of this: every month is recomputed by hand, every error is repeated until noticed, and the accounts, tax, and audit machinery downstream receives nothing.</p><p><b>The custody split.</b> Three owners share the payroll function, and the split is held throughout the track:</p><p>• <b>HR</b> owns who is employed, on what terms, from when — the Employee records and the approved pay decisions.<br>• <b>The payroll operator</b> (this certification) owns the machinery: components, structures, assignments, the run, the slips, the registers.<br>• <b>The accountant</b> owns the numbers' policy: the tax table, the statutory rates, the account wiring — module 2's territory.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Employee record's payroll fields. Chapter 3: Salary Components. Chapter 4: the Salary Structure. Chapter 5: the Salary Structure Assignment. Chapter 6: formulas, conditions, and payment days. Chapter 7: the preview procedure. Chapter 8: the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Employee record: the payroll fields", "12", "<p>Open an <b>Employee</b> record (awesome bar → Employee). HR owns the record; the payroll operator verifies the fields the pay machinery reads. Walk them in order.</p><p><b>1. Company.</b> The employing entity. On a multi-company site, the employee's slips, postings, and registers all belong to this company — the legal-separation rule reaching payroll.</p><p><b>2. Date of Joining.</b> The first month's proration reads this date (Chapter 6). A wrong joining date pays a wrong first month.</p><p><b>3. Status and Relieving Date.</b> Status Active means the run picks the employee up; Left with a Relieving Date means the final month prorates to that date and later runs exclude them (module 7 covers exits in full). The run selects by these fields — an exit not recorded here is a leaver who keeps getting paid.</p><p><b>4. Salary Mode, and the bank fields.</b> Salary Mode = Bank, Cash, or Cheque. For Bank: the <b>Bank Name</b> and <b>Bank A/C No.</b> fields feed the payment run. Rules:</p><p>• Bank details are entered from the employee's own written instruction, filed as an attachment on the record.<br>• Changes to bank details are governed master-data events — verified with the employee through a known channel, never actioned from a forwarded message. This is the same rule the Accounts track applies to supplier bank details, for the same fraud.</p><p><b>5. Payroll Cost Center.</b> The address the employee's salary cost posts to — the branch or unit. Set per employee (or defaulted from the Department where the site is configured so). This one field is what makes the branch P&amp;L's salary line true; an employee without it posts into the company fallback and distorts every branch read.</p><p><b>6. Holiday List.</b> The employee's working calendar. Payment-days proration (Chapter 6) counts working days from this list; leave machinery (the HR track) shares it. Confirm every employee carries the correct list — the branch that works Saturdays needs its own.</p><p><b>7. The tax and statutory fields.</b> The state of residence (module 2's PAYE split reads it), the pension fund administrator and PFA account number (module 2's remittance schedule reads them), and the tax identifiers. Module 2 walks each at its station.</p><p><b>The verification procedure.</b> Before any employee's first run:</p><p>1. Open the Employee record.<br>2. Confirm fields 1-7 against the offer letter and the employee's forms.<br>3. Attach the source documents to the record.<br>4. Note the verification in the record's comments: date, by whom.</p><p>Practical rule: the run inherits every error this walk would have caught. Five minutes per joiner; module 3's checklist assumes it was done.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Salary Component", "13", "<p>Open <b>Salary Component</b> (awesome bar) &gt; New. One component is one pay element, and its fields decide how it computes, how it taxes, and where it posts. Walk the fields.</p><p><b>1. Name and Abbreviation.</b> The name prints on every payslip (write it as the employee should read it: <i>Housing Allowance</i>, not <i>HRA2</i>). The <b>abbreviation</b> is the handle formulas use (Chapter 6): Basic's abbreviation <b>B</b> lets another component compute <i>B * 0.1</i>. Set abbreviations short and stable; changing one later breaks every formula that references it.</p><p><b>2. Type: Earning or Deduction.</b> Earnings add to gross; deductions subtract toward net. The statutory deductions (module 2) are Deduction-type components.</p><p><b>3. The calculation block.</b> Either a fixed <b>Amount</b>, or <b>Amount based on formula</b> with the <b>Formula</b> field (Chapter 6 teaches the writing), plus an optional <b>Condition</b> that must be true for the component to apply at all. A component with neither amount nor formula takes its value from the Salary Structure's row (Chapter 4) — the per-structure override pattern.</p><p><b>4. Is Tax Applicable.</b> Ticked, the component's amount counts toward taxable income; clear, it is exempt. This flag is set per component <b>from the accountant's ruling</b> — which allowances are taxable is tax policy, not operator preference, and the 2025 reform's changes land exactly here at the annual re-read (module 2).</p><p><b>5. Depends on Payment Days.</b> Ticked, the component prorates with the month's payable days — the joiner from the 16th earns roughly half; unpaid leave reduces it (Chapter 6's mechanics). Clear, the component pays flat regardless. Salary-like earnings tick it; fixed reimbursements usually do not.</p><p><b>6. Do Not Include in Total / Statistical Component.</b> Two visibility controls: a <b>Statistical Component</b> computes but pays nothing — used as a base figure other formulas reference; <b>Do Not Include in Total</b> shows a line without moving net pay — module 2 uses it for the employer pension share, which the employee should see but does not pay.</p><p><b>7. The Accounts table.</b> One row per company: the component's ledger account. Earnings point at their expense accounts (Salary expense, split by component family where the chart wants it); statutory deductions point at their <b>custody accounts</b> — PAYE Payable, Pension Payable (module 2 wires each). This table is where payroll meets the Accounts track: the run's accrual Journal Entry posts every component to the account named here, addressed by the employee's Payroll Cost Center. A component with a missing or wrong account row mis-posts every month for every employee that carries it.</p><p><b>The component register.</b> Keep the one-page list: every component with its type, flags, formula, account, and the ruling that set Is Tax Applicable. Reviewed at module 8's annual walk; every new component is a governed addition to this page, not a keyboard improvisation.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The Salary Structure", "12", "<p>Open <b>Salary Structure</b> (awesome bar) &gt; New. A structure is a named pay-package template: which components, in which arrangement, for one family of employees. Walk the form.</p><p><b>1. Name and Company.</b> Name structures for the package, not the person: <i>Head Office Staff</i>, <i>Branch Retail Staff</i>, <i>Management</i>. One structure serves every employee on that package; a structure per person is Chapter 9's Pattern 2.</p><p><b>2. Payroll Frequency.</b> Monthly for the standard Nigerian salary cycle. The frequency governs the run's periods.</p><p><b>3. The Earnings table.</b> One row per earning component. Each row carries the component and — where the component itself has no formula — the row-level amount or formula. The standard Nigerian shape enters here as component rows computed from the base (Chapter 5's assignment supplies the base):</p><p>• <b>Basic</b> — commonly a percentage of the base (formula on the assignment's base).<br>• <b>Housing</b> and <b>Transport</b> — percentages likewise. The Basic + Housing + Transport family matters beyond presentation: module 2's pension computes on it, so the split is the accountant's decision, recorded.<br>• The remaining allowances per the package — each an existing component from Chapter 3's register.</p><p><b>4. The Deductions table.</b> The deduction components this package carries: the statutory set (PAYE, employee pension, NHF where applicable — module 2 builds each) plus any package-specific deductions. Because the statutory components carry their own formulas and flags, the structure usually just lists them — the computation lives on the component, the membership lives on the structure.</p><p><b>5. The payment and posting defaults.</b> The structure carries the Mode of Payment and payment-account defaults the run will use, plus leave-encashment settings where the leave policy uses them (the HR track's territory; noted here for the field walk).</p><p><b>6. Submit.</b> Structures are submittable documents: submitted, they can be assigned; amendments follow the document rules — a change to a live structure is a governed event (module 6 covers pay changes properly; module 8 covers structure versioning).</p><p><b>The design procedure.</b></p><p>1. List the pay packages that actually exist — usually three to six families, not one per person.<br>2. Confirm the component set and the B/H/T split per package with the accountant.<br>3. Build each structure from registered components only.<br>4. Preview one slip per structure (Chapter 7) before any assignment goes live.</p><p>Practical rule: the structure count is a health number. Three structures for thirty staff is a design; twenty-eight structures for thirty staff is a spreadsheet wearing a costume of records — merge them by package and carry the differences on the assignment's base.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The Salary Structure Assignment", "12", "<p>Open <b>Salary Structure Assignment</b> (awesome bar) &gt; New. The assignment is the record that joins one employee to one structure from one date, and carries the number the formulas compute from. Walk the fields.</p><p><b>1. Employee and Salary Structure.</b> The person and the package template. One employee has one <b>active</b> assignment at a time; a new assignment from a later date supersedes the old from that date — which is exactly how pay rises are recorded (module 6): not by editing history, but by a new assignment from the effective date.</p><p><b>2. From Date.</b> When this assignment starts governing. Rules:</p><p>• The first assignment's From Date aligns with the Date of Joining (or the pay decision's effective date).<br>• From Dates are honest dates. A rise effective the 1st is dated the 1st, even if entered on the 20th — the run computes from the record, and module 3's run for that month will apply it correctly.</p><p><b>3. Base.</b> The assignment's central field: the employee's base amount, from which the structure's percentage formulas compute. The base is <b>the pay decision, as a number</b> — it comes from the offer letter or the approved review (module 6), it is entered once here, and every component that says <i>base * something</i> reads it. Two employees on the same structure with different bases produce different slips from one template: this is how three structures serve thirty staff.</p><p><b>4. Variable.</b> The second reference amount, available to formulas as the variable-pay anchor where the package uses one (module 5's territory).</p><p><b>5. Income Tax Slab.</b> The assignment links the employee to the <b>Income Tax Slab</b> that computes their PAYE — module 2, Chapter 2 builds the slab; this field is where it attaches. An assignment without its slab produces a slip without its tax: the preview (Chapter 7) checks for exactly this.</p><p><b>6. Company and Currency.</b> Per the employee's record; the naira for this track's scope.</p><p><b>The assignment procedures.</b></p><p>• <b>The joiner:</b> Employee verified (Chapter 2) → assignment created with structure, From Date, Base from the offer letter, slab attached → preview (Chapter 7) → filed.<br>• <b>The pay change:</b> new assignment from the effective date with the new base; the old assignment stands as history. Never edit an old assignment's base — that rewrites what past months were computed from, and the register stops matching the slips it produced.<br>• <b>The bulk load at go-live:</b> assignments imported through the Data Import tool under the sysadmin track's six-step governed procedure, then <b>every</b> structure previewed and a sample of individual slips checked against the old payroll's last month.</p><p>Practical rule: the assignment list, filtered by structure, is the answer to \"who is on what package at what base\" — the pay register in one view. Module 6's governance reads it; keep it clean.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Formulas, conditions, and payment days", "13", "<p>The computation layer. Three mechanics, each with its procedure.</p><p><b>Formulas.</b> A component's Formula field computes its amount each run. The writing rules:</p><p>1. <b>Reference by abbreviation and by base.</b> <i>base * 0.4</i> computes forty percent of the assignment's Base. <i>B * 0.1</i> computes ten percent of the component whose abbreviation is B — component-on-component references, which is how pension computes on Basic + Housing + Transport: <i>(B + H + T) * 0.08</i> (module 2 builds it).<br>2. <b>Order matters through dependency:</b> a formula can only reference components that compute before it. Keep the dependency shallow — base-referencing formulas for the earnings, family-sum formulas for the statutory set — and the evaluation stays readable.<br>3. <b>Write the formula on the component when it is universal</b> (pension's rate is law, the same everywhere) <b>and on the structure's row when it is per-package</b> (Basic's percentage differs by package). One home per formula; a formula duplicated in both places is a future contradiction.</p><p><b>Conditions.</b> The Condition field gates whether the component applies at all: a component conditioned on <i>base &gt; 0</i> always applies; one conditioned on a field or threshold applies selectively (module 2 uses a condition where a levy applies only above a threshold; module 5 uses them for variable pay). The rule: conditions encode <b>written policy</b> — every condition traces to a line on the policy page, because a condition nobody can explain is a deduction nobody can defend.</p><p><b>Payment days — the proration engine.</b> For components with <b>Depends on Payment Days</b> ticked, the slip computes: <i>payment days = working days in the period (from the employee's Holiday List) − unmarked absences and leave without pay (from the leave machinery)</i>, and the component pays <i>amount × payment days ÷ working days</i>. The three standing cases:</p><p>• <b>The mid-month joiner:</b> Date of Joining on the 16th → payment days run from the 16th → the salary-like components pay their fraction automatically. No manual arithmetic; the record computed it.<br>• <b>The mid-month leaver:</b> Relieving Date the 12th → the final slip prorates to the 12th (module 7 completes the exit).<br>• <b>Leave without pay:</b> approved LWP days reduce payment days — the HR track's leave records feeding this track's slips. The operator's check: the slip's payment-days figure against the month's known absences, per module 3's run checklist.</p><p><b>The verification habit.</b> Every formula, condition, and proration rule is verified the same way: <b>one previewed slip</b> (Chapter 7) whose arithmetic is checked by hand once. After that single hand-check, the record computes every future month — which is the pipeline's whole promise, and why the hand-check is never skipped on the first pass.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The preview procedure", "12", "<p>No structure pays anyone before a previewed slip has been read against the source documents. This chapter is that procedure — the track's equivalent of the Accounts track's posting-map verification.</p><p><b>Previewing one slip.</b></p><p>1. Open <b>Salary Slip</b> (awesome bar) &gt; New.<br>2. Select the Employee and the payroll period. The form pulls the employee's active assignment, its structure, and its slab, and computes: every earning row, every deduction row, the payment days, gross, total deductions, net.<br>3. <b>Do not submit.</b> The preview is read and discarded (or saved as draft and deleted after) — module 3's run generates the real slips. The preview exists to be checked, not to pay.</p><p><b>The reading, line by line.</b></p><p>1. <b>Earnings against the offer letter:</b> each component present, each amount matching the letter's package arithmetic. The Basic/Housing/Transport split at the agreed percentages of the base.<br>2. <b>Deductions against the accountant's table:</b> PAYE present and plausible against the slab (module 2's Chapter 2 gives the plausibility check), pension at its rate on the right family, the other statutory lines per applicability.<br>3. <b>Payment days against the calendar:</b> full month = full days; the joiner's fraction correct.<br>4. <b>Net pay stated back:</b> gross − deductions = net, and the net against what the employee was told they would receive. A package quoted net in the offer letter is reconciled to its gross here — <b>before</b> the first payday conversation, not during it.<br>5. <b>One hand-check:</b> one formula-computed line recomputed manually (Chapter 6's rule). One line suffices; it proves the chain.</p><p><b>When to preview.</b></p><p>• Every new structure, before its first assignment — one preview per structure at a representative base.<br>• Every joiner, at assignment — their own preview against their own letter.<br>• Every pay change — the new assignment previewed before its first run.<br>• Every component or formula change — one preview per affected structure (module 8's change discipline; the sysadmin track's test-then-live logic applied to pay).<br>• After the go-live bulk load — every structure, plus a sample of individuals against the old payroll's last month.</p><p><b>The record.</b> Each preview gets one line in the payroll log: employee/structure, date, checked against what, by whom, result. Findings are fixed <b>in the records</b> (the component, the row, the assignment) and re-previewed — never patched on the slip, because a slip patch fixes one month and leaves the record wrong for every month after.</p><p>Practical rule: the preview costs five minutes and runs before money moves; the same error found on payday costs a correction run, a bank recall attempt, and an employee's trust. Every module downstream assumes the preview happened.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's structures", "13", "<p>HealthTrade builds its pay machinery. Every chapter appears. (HealthTrade is the pharmaceutical distributor the whole programme follows — three branches, Head Office, about thirty staff.)</p><p><b>Step 1 — the employee walk (Chapter 2).</b> All employee records are verified against files: two records carry no <b>Payroll Cost Center</b> (their salary cost had been landing in the company fallback — the Accounts track's unaddressed check had already flagged the naira; this walk names the cause), one bank account number fails its verification and is corrected from the employee's written instruction, and every record gets its Holiday List confirmed — the retail branches on the Saturdays-working list, Head Office on the standard list.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the component register (Chapter 3).</b> Built with the accountant in one sitting: the earnings set (Basic, Housing, Transport, plus a Meal and a Utility allowance) with <b>Is Tax Applicable</b> ruled per line and the accounts table pointed at the salary expense accounts; the deduction set left as stubs for module 2's statutory build. Abbreviations set short (B, H, T) because the pension formula will reference them. The register page is written: every component, its flags, its account, its ruling.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the structures (Chapter 4).</b> The package census finds <b>three families</b>, not thirty: <i>Branch Retail Staff</i>, <i>Head Office Staff</i>, <i>Management</i>. Three Salary Structures are built, the B/H/T split confirmed with the accountant (recorded: the split feeds pension), the earnings rows carrying per-package percentages of base, the deduction membership listed.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the assignments (Chapter 5).</b> Thirty Salary Structure Assignments load through the governed import: employee, structure, From Date, <b>Base from each offer letter</b>. The assignment list filtered by structure becomes the standing pay register — who is on what package at what base, one view.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the previews (Chapters 6-7).</b> One preview per structure at a representative base, then a five-employee sample against the old spreadsheet payroll's last month. The previews catch two findings before anyone is paid: a Transport formula on the Retail structure written as <i>base * 0.15</i> where the letter said ten percent — a typing error that would have overpaid eleven staff monthly — and one mid-2025 joiner whose old spreadsheet had never prorated the first month (the spreadsheet had paid the full month; the slip's payment-days figure computes the truth, and the difference is documented for the file rather than clawed back — the owner's call, recorded). Both fixes land in the records — the structure row corrected, re-previewed, logged.</p><p><b>The state at module's end.</b> Three structures, thirty verified assignments, a component register with rulings, and a preview log with two findings closed. The statutory deductions are stubs awaiting module 2 — which is exactly where the track goes next. The payroll operator's note in the log: <i>the spreadsheet computed pay thirty times a month; the records now compute it once, per rule.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the first law", "11", "<p>The structure layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the spreadsheet beside the system.</b> Symptom: pay computed in a spreadsheet, one lump posted monthly; no slips, no register, no proration, no audit trail; every downstream module starved. Cause: the pipeline never built (Chapter 1). Fix: this module, run in order — the employee walk, the component register, the structures, the assignments, the previews — with the sample-against-last-month check making the cutover safe.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the structure per person.</b> Symptom: as many structures as staff; every pay change a structure edit; no package view. Cause: the base misunderstood — differences that belong on the assignment's Base built as separate templates (Chapters 4-5). Fix: the package census, structures merged to the real families, differences moved to bases, and the structure count watched as a health number.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the edited history.</b> Symptom: pay rises made by editing the old assignment's base; the register no longer matching the slips it produced; last year's figures silently different. Cause: the supersession rule unknown (Chapter 5). Fix: new assignment from the effective date, always; old assignments as history, never edited; module 6's governance making the rule structural.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the unverified formula.</b> Symptom: a percentage typed wrong paying every carrier of the structure wrong, monthly, until an employee complains. Cause: the preview skipped (Chapters 6-7). Fix: the preview procedure at every trigger — new structure, joiner, change, go-live — with its one hand-check and its log line.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the unaddressed salary.</b> Symptom: the branch P&amp;L's salary line untrue; payroll cost pooled in the company fallback. Cause: Payroll Cost Center blank on employee records (Chapter 2). Fix: the field on the joiner checklist, the existing records swept, and the Accounts track's monthly unaddressed check standing guard.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the slip patch.</b> Symptom: errors \"fixed\" by editing the month's slip; the same error returning next month; records and slips diverging. Cause: fixes made downstream of the fault (Chapter 7). Fix: the rule — findings are fixed in the records and re-previewed; the slip is output, never the place of repair.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: recite the seven-step pipeline and the custody split; walk the Employee record's seven payroll fields and run the joiner verification; create a Salary Component and explain each flag — Is Tax Applicable, Depends on Payment Days, Statistical, Do Not Include in Total — and the Accounts table; build a Salary Structure with its earnings and deductions tables and defend the structure count; create a Salary Structure Assignment with Base, From Date, and the slab link, and state the supersession rule; write a base formula and a component-family formula, and compute a joiner's payment days; and run the preview procedure with its five reads and its log line.</p><p><b>The first law: pay is computed by the records — components carry their accounts, structures carry the package, assignments carry the base, and every slip previews before it pays.</b> Module 2 builds the statutory deductions: PAYE, pension, and the Nigerian compliance set.</p>"),
]

