# ============================ PAYROLL 2 ============================
LESSONS_P2 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The statutory layer: custody components", "11", "<p>This module builds the statutory deductions in ZhiftERP: the Income Tax Slab and PAYE, pension, NHF and the levy family, the state-of-residence split, the account wiring and the posting read, the remittance workflow, and the Salary Register report.</p><p><b>The custody frame, carried in.</b> The Accounts track's fifth law governs this module from the other side: amounts <b>withheld from the employee</b> — PAYE, the employee pension share, NHF — are custody money, held for an authority, and amounts <b>borne by the employer</b> — the employer pension share, NSITF, ITF — are the business's own cost. In the machinery, both are Salary Components; the difference is carried by the component's type and flags, and by which account its Accounts table names:</p><p>• Employee-withheld amounts: <b>Deduction</b>-type components, posting to <b>custody accounts</b> (PAYE Payable, Pension Payable, NHF Payable — Tax-type leaves the Accounts track created).<br>• Employer-borne amounts: components that show without reducing net (Chapter 3's mechanics), posting expense against the matching payable.</p><p><b>The division of authority, stated for this module.</b> The rates, bands, reliefs, thresholds, and applicability rules are <b>the accountant's table</b> — statute, read from current law, re-read annually and at every reform. The 2025 tax reform re-shaped the personal income tax structure; this manual therefore teaches <b>no rates as facts</b>. It teaches the machinery: where the accountant's numbers are entered, how the components compute from them, and how the results are proven. The operator's competence is the machinery; the operator's discipline is refusing to improvise a rate.</p><p><b>The statutory set, previewed.</b> The components this module builds, each at its chapter:</p><p>1. <b>PAYE</b> — the income tax deduction, computed by the Income Tax Slab (Chapter 2).<br>2. <b>Pension</b> — employee and employer shares on the B/H/T family (Chapter 3).<br>3. <b>NHF, NSITF, ITF</b> — the housing fund and the employer levies, per applicability (Chapter 4).<br>4. The <b>state split</b> for PAYE remittance (Chapter 5).</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Income Tax Slab and the PAYE component. Chapter 3: pension. Chapter 4: NHF and the levies. Chapter 5: the state-of-residence split. Chapter 6: account wiring and the posting read. Chapter 7: the remittance workflow. Chapter 8: the Salary Register. Chapter 9: the HealthTrade case. Chapter 10: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Income Tax Slab and the PAYE component", "13", "<p>PAYE in ZhiftERP is computed by two records working together: the <b>Income Tax Slab</b> (the accountant's table, entered once) and the <b>PAYE Salary Component</b> (the deduction that applies it). Build them in that order.</p><p><b>Building the Income Tax Slab.</b></p><p>1. Open <b>Income Tax Slab</b> (awesome bar) &gt; New.<br>2. Name it for its source and effective date — <i>PAYE — [year] table</i> — and set the Company, Currency, and <b>Effective From</b> date.<br>3. Enter the <b>Standard Tax Exemption Amount</b> where the current law's relief structure maps to one — the consolidated relief and exemption figures come from the accountant's current computation sheet, entered as the law states them at entry time.<br>4. Enter the <b>slab rows:</b> each row is an income band with its percentage — the graduated bands from the accountant's table, transcribed exactly. Conditions on rows serve where the law's structure needs them; the accountant reads the built slab against their table before it is used.<br>5. Submit. A new law or reform means a <b>new slab</b> with its own Effective From — never an edit to the old one, which computed history.</p><p><b>Building the PAYE component.</b></p><p>1. Open Salary Component &gt; New: name <i>PAYE</i>, Type = Deduction.<br>2. Tick the component's income-tax flag (<b>Variable Based On Taxable Salary</b>) — this is what tells the run to compute the amount from the employee's slab and taxable income rather than from a formula.<br>3. Accounts table: the company row pointing at <b>PAYE Payable</b> (Chapter 6 verifies the posting).<br>4. Add the component to every structure's Deductions table (module 1, Chapter 4).</p><p><b>Attaching the slab.</b> The slab links on each employee's <b>Salary Structure Assignment</b> (module 1, Chapter 5). The run then computes each employee's PAYE from: their taxable earnings (the components with Is Tax Applicable ticked — module 1's flag now doing its work), less the slab's exemptions, through the slab's bands, divided to the monthly figure.</p><p><b>The plausibility check — the operator's proof.</b> For one previewed slip:</p><p>1. Sum the taxable earnings (the flagged components only).<br>2. Run the sum through the accountant's paper computation for one employee — the accountant's sheet is the reference, the slip is the candidate.<br>3. They must agree. Disagreement means a flag is wrong (a component taxable that should be exempt, or the reverse), a slab row mis-transcribed, or the exemption figure wrong — all record fixes, found on one slip before thirty slips inherit them.</p><p>Practical rule: the slab is re-read every year and at every reform announcement — the accountant confirms it still matches law, and a change produces a new dated slab attached by new assignments. The slab that quietly drifted from statute is a compliance failure computed automatically, thirty times a month.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Pension: two shares, one family", "12", "<p>The contributory pension deducts an employee share and adds an employer share, both computed on the Basic + Housing + Transport family. The current statutory rates come from the accountant's table; the build is the same at any rates.</p><p><b>The employee share.</b></p><p>1. Salary Component &gt; New: <i>Pension — Employee</i>, Type = Deduction.<br>2. <b>Formula:</b> <i>(B + H + T) * [the employee rate]</i> — the abbreviations module 1 set on Basic, Housing, and Transport now earning their keep. The rate figure is entered from the accountant's table and noted on the component register with its source.<br>3. <b>Is Tax Applicable consequence:</b> pension contributions carry tax treatment (deductible against taxable income under the law's rules) — the accountant rules how the slab's exemption structure and the component flags interact, and the Chapter 2 plausibility check proves the combination on one slip.<br>4. Accounts table: <b>Pension Payable</b>.</p><p><b>The employer share.</b> The employer's contribution is the business's cost, not the employee's deduction — it must appear (the employee should see the full contribution going to their fund) without reducing net pay:</p><p>1. Salary Component &gt; New: <i>Pension — Employer</i>, configured with the visibility flags from module 1 Chapter 3 — shown on the slip, excluded from the net computation (<b>Do Not Include in Total</b>), and computed by the same family formula at the employer rate.<br>2. Accounts: the employer share posts pension expense against <b>Pension Payable</b> — so the payable account accumulates <b>both shares</b>, which is the figure the remittance pays (Chapter 7).</p><p><b>The PFA dimension.</b> Remittance is per employee, to <b>each employee's own fund administrator</b>. The machinery:</p><p>• The Employee record carries the PFA and the RSA (retirement savings account) number — module 1's Chapter 2 walk verified them; a joiner without a PFA is chased at joining, not at remittance.<br>• The remittance schedule (Chapter 7) is the month's pension figures <b>split by PFA</b> — the Salary Register's export grouped on the PFA field (Chapter 8), each PFA's schedule listing its employees with RSA numbers and both shares.</p><p><b>The timing rule, carried from the Accounts track.</b> Pension remittance is pinned to <b>days from paying salaries</b> — the tightest window in the statutory family. The calendar line reads: salaries paid on the 25th → pension schedules and payments out within the statutory number of days, owned by name. Chapter 7 places it in the workflow.</p><p>Practical rule: the pension preview check is two multiplications — the family sum times each rate — done once by hand on one slip. The B/H/T split (module 1's recorded accountant decision) is what those multiplications stand on: a structure that quietly changed its split has changed every pension amount, which is why the split is a governed number.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — NHF and the employer levies", "12", "<p>The remaining statutory family: one employee deduction and the employer levies. Each is a component built the same way; each begins with an <b>applicability ruling</b> from the accountant, because these obligations attach by headcount, sector, and election under the current law.</p><p><b>NHF — the housing fund.</b></p><p>1. <b>The ruling first:</b> whether NHF applies to this business's employees, and at what rate on what base (historically a percentage of Basic; the current position is the accountant's line). Where the law makes participation elective for some categories, the election is documented per employee, and the component's <b>Condition</b> field (module 1, Chapter 6) gates it accordingly.<br>2. Salary Component &gt; New: <i>NHF</i>, Type = Deduction, Formula on the ruled base — <i>B * [rate]</i> — Accounts table pointing at <b>NHF Payable</b>.<br>3. Added to the structures whose staff it covers.</p><p><b>NSITF — the employees' compensation levy.</b> Employer-borne: a percentage of payroll per the ruling.</p><p>1. Configured as an employer-cost component (the Chapter 3 employer pattern: visible or statistical per the business's slip-presentation choice, excluded from net) or — where the business prefers — computed off-slip by the accountant's monthly journal from the Salary Register's totals. Either home is lawful; <b>one</b> home is chosen and recorded, because a levy computed in both places pays twice.<br>2. Accounts: NSITF expense against <b>NSITF Payable</b>.</p><p><b>ITF — the training fund.</b> Employer-borne, applicability by headcount/turnover thresholds per the ruling — the <b>Condition</b> field or the off-slip route, same one-home rule, posting to <b>ITF Payable</b>.</p><p><b>The applicability register.</b> One page, kept with the component register: each statutory line, the ruling (applies / does not apply / applies to these categories), the rate and base, the ruling's source and date, and the component or journal that implements it. Two reads use this page:</p><p>1. <b>The annual re-read</b> — every ruling re-confirmed against current law with the accountant; the 2025 reform's consolidation makes this read the year's most important ten minutes.<br>2. <b>The threshold watch</b> — obligations that attach by headcount are re-checked when the business grows: the levy that did not apply at eleven staff may apply at twenty-six, and the applicability register is where that question lives instead of in nobody's memory.</p><p>Practical rule: a statutory component with no ruling behind it is an improvisation, whichever direction it errs — deducting what the law does not require injures the employee; skipping what it does require accrues penalties. The page-before-the-component order is absolute in this chapter.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The state-of-residence split", "12", "<p>PAYE remits to <b>the state of each employee's residence</b> — one business, several state authorities, one calendar. The machinery is one field, one grouping, and one discipline.</p><p><b>The field.</b> The Employee record carries the residential address, and with it the <b>state of residence</b> — verified at joining from the employee's declared address (module 1's Chapter 2 walk), and updated as a governed event when the employee moves (the employee's written notice, filed on the record; the change effective from the notice for future remittances).</p><p><b>The grouping.</b> The month's PAYE total is not one figure — it is a set of figures, one per state with resident employees. The mechanics:</p><p>1. The run computes each employee's PAYE (Chapter 2's machinery).<br>2. The <b>Salary Register</b> (Chapter 8) exports the month's slips with the employee dimension — grouped or pivoted on the state field, it produces the per-state schedule: each state's employees, their PAYE amounts, the state subtotal.<br>3. Each state's remittance (Chapter 7) pays its subtotal to that state's authority, with the schedule as the evidence behind the payment.</p><p><b>The standing example.</b> A Lagos head office with Ogun-resident staff files in <b>both</b> states: Lagos receives the Lagos residents' PAYE, Ogun the Ogun residents' — the office's own location is not the test; the employees' residence is. A three-branch business spanning states multiplies the schedule accordingly, and the register's grouping produces all of it from the same run.</p><p><b>The discipline — why the field is governed.</b> The state field is a remittance-routing field: wrong, it sends an employee's tax to the wrong authority, which surfaces later as the right authority's demand for tax \"never received\" plus the wrong authority's money to recover — two conversations, both slow. Hence:</p><p>1. The field is <b>mandatory at joining</b> — the verification walk refuses a blank.<br>2. Changes are <b>evidenced</b> — the employee's notice, filed.<br>3. The annual sweep — once a year, the state field列 is exported and each employee confirms their state in the same pass as the payslip-details confirmation (module 4's communication routines carry it) — catches the silent movers.</p><p><b>The registration prerequisite, flagged to the accountant.</b> Remitting to a state requires the business to be registered with that state's authority. The operator's job is the <b>flag</b>: the first month a new state appears in the schedule (a new joiner resident somewhere new), the accountant is told before remittance day, because the registration is their process and it takes longer than a payment.</p><p>Practical rule: the per-state schedule is produced monthly even in the months it looks identical — the schedule is the remittance's evidence, and the month it changes silently (a mover, a joiner) is exactly the month a from-memory remittance goes wrong.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Account wiring and the posting read", "12", "<p>Every statutory component posts to the ledger through its Accounts table. This chapter wires the accounts and proves the posting — the Accounts track's posting-map verification, performed for payroll.</p><p><b>The wiring map.</b> Confirm each component's Accounts table row (per company):</p><p>• <b>Earnings components</b> → the salary expense accounts (split by component family where the chart design wants Basic and allowances read separately).<br>• <b>PAYE</b> → PAYE Payable. <b>Pension (both shares)</b> → Pension Payable (the employer share's expense side to pension expense). <b>NHF</b> → NHF Payable. <b>NSITF / ITF</b> → their payables against their expenses.<br>• Every payable named here is a <b>Tax-type custody leaf</b> the Accounts track created — if one is missing from the chart, it is created there first (typed, named plainly), then wired here.</p><p><b>What the run posts.</b> The Payroll Entry (module 3) books the month's payroll as an accrual <b>Journal Entry</b>: debit the expense accounts (salary expense by component, addressed by each employee's Payroll Cost Center), credit the custody payables (PAYE, pension, NHF at their withheld amounts), credit the <b>net-pay liability</b> (the amount owed to employees, cleared by the payment run). The employer-borne lines debit their expenses and credit their payables in the same posting.</p><p><b>The posting read — the one-time proof, then the monthly confirmation.</b></p><p>1. After the first run (module 3), open the run's accrual Journal Entry and use <b>View &gt; Accounting Ledger</b>.<br>2. Read the rows against the wiring map above: each expense line present at the register's totals, each custody credit at the register's deduction totals, the net-pay credit at the register's net total, every P&amp;L row carrying its cost center.<br>3. Any mismatch is a wiring fault — the component's Accounts row corrected, the posting corrected by the Accounts track's rules, the read repeated.<br>4. Thereafter the read is the <b>payroll tie</b>, monthly, in minutes: register column totals = posting lines = custody account movements (the General Ledger report per account) = zero after remittance. The Accounts track runs this tie from its side; this chapter is where the payroll operator makes it true from theirs.</p><p><b>The cost-center consequence, restated.</b> The posting carries each employee's Payroll Cost Center onto the expense rows — which is the entire mechanism behind a true branch salary line. The module 1 walk that filled the field, and the Accounts track's unaddressed check that watches the fallback, meet here.</p><p>Practical rule: the wiring is verified <b>before</b> the first live run and re-verified whenever a component is added or its account row touched — one preview posting read against the map. A wrong account row mis-posts silently, every month, for every employee; the read costs ten minutes once.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The remittance workflow", "12", "<p>The custody accounts fill at the run and must empty at remittance. This chapter is the monthly workflow — the Accounts track's six-step filing routine, executed from the payroll chair.</p><p><b>The workflow, per custody, in order.</b></p><p>1. <b>Close the register.</b> The month's <b>Salary Register</b> (Chapter 8) produced and filed — the document-level evidence every schedule derives from.<br>2. <b>Produce the schedules.</b> From the register's export: the <b>per-state PAYE schedule</b> (Chapter 5), the <b>per-PFA pension schedule</b> with RSA numbers and both shares (Chapter 3), the NHF schedule, the levy computations. Each schedule's total is written beside its custody account's balance — they must agree, which is the payroll tie's remittance face.<br>3. <b>Prepare the payments.</b> One <b>Payment Entry</b> per authority per obligation: paid from the <b>tax-reserve bank account</b> (the Accounts track's reserve — payroll custodies are swept there at the run, per that track's schedule), debiting the custody payable, with the schedule reference in the entry and the schedule attached.<br>4. <b>Release under the separation.</b> The preparer never releases (the Accounts track's rule 1 of bank governance); the payroll operator prepares, the release authority releases.<br>5. <b>File by the dates.</b> The calendar's lines, each owned by name: PAYE to each state by its day; <b>pension inside its days-from-salary window</b> — the tightest line, pinned to the actual salary payment date, not the month's end; NHF and the levies per their cycles. Where a portal filing accompanies the payment, the filing is made from the schedule (one source), and the acknowledgment joins the file.<br>6. <b>Verify the zero.</b> After the cycle, each custody account's balance returns to zero (the General Ledger report per account, or the Trial Balance line). A custody that will not zero is telling one of the Accounts track's four stories — a skipped tie, a short or misposted payment, prior residue, or custody spent — and the payroll operator reads the same diagnostic from this side.</p><p><b>The evidence bundle.</b> Per custody per month, one folder: the register extract, the schedule, the Payment Entry, the receipt or acknowledgment, the zero check. The certificate-and-receipt file is what answers the employee's \"was my pension actually paid?\" — with their PFA's schedule line and the payment's receipt, in one look — and what answers the authority's query the same way.</p><p><b>The penalty line.</b> The Accounts track's rule closes this chapter unchanged: penalties on late statutory remittance are the most expensive credit line available, taken by default. The calendar with named owners, the reserve funded at the run, and the zero check every cycle are the three lines that keep the penalty figure at its target: zero, watched monthly.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — The Salary Register", "12", "<p>Open the <b>Salary Register</b> report (awesome bar). One row per salary slip for the selected period and filters, with the components as columns — the month's payroll on one screen, and the source of every schedule this module produces.</p><p><b>The filters.</b> Company; the period (from/to); Employee or Department where a subset is wanted; and the docstatus filter — the standing read is <b>submitted slips only</b>, because draft slips are computations, not payroll. Run it after every run closes (module 3), and file the month's copy in the payroll folder.</p><p><b>The columns and the reads.</b> The register shows, per employee: the earnings components, the deductions components, gross, total deduction, net — plus the loan and advance columns where those features run (module 5's territory). The standing reads:</p><p>1. <b>The column totals</b> — the figures the whole compliance chain hangs on: total PAYE (the remittance sum before its state split), total pension by share, total NHF, gross and net. These totals are what Chapter 6's posting read ties to the Journal Entry, and what Chapter 7's schedules must sum back to.<br>2. <b>The month-on-month comparison</b> — this month's register beside last month's: the headcount delta explained by joiners and leavers (module 3's run checklist), each material per-employee movement explained by a documented event (a rise's new assignment, a proration, a variable-pay line). An unexplained movement is a finding <b>before payday</b>, which is the comparison's whole point.<br>3. <b>The export-and-group reads</b> — the register exported and grouped: by <b>state of residence</b> (Chapter 5's PAYE schedules), by <b>PFA</b> (Chapter 3's pension schedules), by <b>Payroll Cost Center</b> (the branch salary read the P&amp;L will confirm).</p><p><b>The confidentiality rule.</b> The register is the most sensitive report on the site: every salary, named. Its governance is the sysadmin track's machinery applied without exception:</p><p>• <b>Access:</b> the payroll operator, the release/review authority, the accountant — role-permissioned, nobody else. The HR-adjacent curiosity read is not a use case.<br>• <b>Exports:</b> each export exists for a schedule or a filing; it is stored in the payroll folder (business-controlled, per the backup-custody logic), never in personal drives or chat threads; and export permission on the payroll doctypes is part of module 2-of-sysadmin's matrix.<br>• <b>The register in meetings:</b> aggregates travel (totals, by cost center); named rows do not, outside the roles above.</p><p>Practical rule: the register is produced <b>once per month as the closed record</b> and every schedule derives from that one export — schedules produced from fresh ad-hoc runs at different moments are how the PAYE schedule and the pension schedule quietly disagree about the same month. One register, many derivations.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Case study: HealthTrade's statutory build", "13", "<p>The statutory layer built onto module 1's structures, and the first full cycle run. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The slab (Chapter 2).</b> The accountant brings the current PAYE computation sheet; the <b>Income Tax Slab</b> is built from it — exemption figure, band rows — named and dated, submitted, and read back against the sheet line by line before use. The <b>PAYE</b> component is created with the taxable-salary flag and wired to PAYE Payable; the slab attaches to all thirty assignments in one governed pass. The plausibility check runs on one Head Office slip: taxable earnings summed, walked through the accountant's paper computation — first pass disagrees by a small monthly amount, traced in twenty minutes to the <b>Meal allowance flagged Is Tax Applicable when the ruling said exempt</b>. The component flag is corrected, the register's ruling column updated, the check re-run: agreement to the naira. One slip's check; thirty slips inherit the fix.</p><p><b>Pension (Chapter 3).</b> <i>Pension — Employee</i> and <i>Pension — Employer</i> built on <i>(B + H + T)</i> at the accountant's rates; the employer share configured visible-but-excluded-from-net; both wired to Pension Payable. The Employee-record walk from module 1 pays off: all thirty carry PFA and RSA numbers except one joiner — chased and captured <b>at joining</b>, not at remittance day. The hand-check: two multiplications on one slip, agreed.</p><p><b>The applicability page (Chapter 4).</b> Written with the accountant in one sitting: NHF ruled per the current position with the covered categories conditioned on the component; NSITF ruled applicable, implemented as the accountant's monthly journal from the register totals (the one-home rule recorded); ITF ruled by the threshold and diarised on the threshold watch. Every line carries its source and date.</p><p><b>The wiring read (Chapter 6).</b> Before the first live run, the preview posting is read against the map — and catches one wiring fault: <b>NHF's account row pointed at PAYE Payable</b> (a copy-paste at component creation). Corrected in the Accounts table, re-read, clean. The fault would have merged two custodies' balances and broken both zero checks, silently, from month one.</p><p><b>The first cycle (Chapters 5, 7, 8).</b> The run closes (module 3's procedure); the <b>Salary Register</b> is produced once and filed. The derivations follow: the state grouping yields <b>two PAYE schedules</b> — Lagos and Ogun (three Ogun-resident staff the residence walk had captured); the PFA grouping yields <b>four pension schedules</b>; NHF's schedule lists its covered staff. Payments prepare from the reserve, release under the separation, file by the calendar — pension inside its days-from-salary window, tracked from the actual payment date. The zero check closes the cycle: PAYE Payable, Pension Payable, NHF Payable all at zero; the month's evidence bundles filed. The accountant's note in the payroll log: <i>the register, the posting, the schedules, and the payments are one set of numbers for the first time — the tie holds at every link.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Common mistakes & the second law", "11", "<p>The statutory layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the improvised rate.</b> Symptom: a percentage typed from memory or a forum; deductions the law does not support, or shortfalls it penalises. Cause: the division of authority ignored (Chapter 1). Fix: the rule made absolute — every rate, band, and ruling enters from the accountant's current table, recorded on the register with source and date, re-read annually and at every reform.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the drifted slab.</b> Symptom: PAYE computed on last regime's bands months after a reform; the compliance failure automated. Cause: the slab treated as set-and-forget (Chapter 2). Fix: the annual re-read on the calendar, reforms producing a <b>new dated slab</b> attached by new assignments, and the plausibility check re-run on one slip at every slab change.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the wrong-flag component.</b> Symptom: the exempt allowance taxed, or the taxable one exempted — every slip wrong by the same small amount. Cause: Is Tax Applicable set by guess (Chapters 1-2). Fix: the flag ruled per component on the register, and the plausibility check — one slip against the accountant's paper computation — at build and at every flag change.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the merged custodies.</b> Symptom: custody accounts that never zero cleanly; two obligations' money indistinguishable. Cause: Accounts-table wiring faults (Chapter 6). Fix: the wiring map verified by the posting read before the first run and at every component change — ten minutes against a silent monthly mis-post.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the from-memory remittance.</b> Symptom: payments made without schedules; the state split guessed; the PFA schedule reconstructed when a fund queries; the register and the payments disagreeing about the same month. Cause: derivations skipped or run from ad-hoc extracts (Chapters 5, 7, 8). Fix: one monthly register as the closed record, every schedule derived from it, every payment carrying its schedule, and the zero check closing each cycle.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the leaked register.</b> Symptom: the salary register in personal drives and chat threads; every salary effectively public. Cause: the confidentiality rule unenforced (Chapter 8). Fix: role-permissioned access, exports only for schedules and filings into the business-controlled folder, aggregates-not-names in meetings, and the export permission in the sysadmin matrix.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the custody frame and the division of authority; build an Income Tax Slab from the accountant's sheet, create the PAYE component with its taxable-salary flag, attach the slab on assignments, and run the plausibility check; build both pension shares on the B/H/T family with the employer share visible-but-excluded, and produce the per-PFA schedule; keep the applicability register with its annual re-read and threshold watch; maintain the state field and produce the per-state schedule; verify the wiring by the posting read and run the monthly payroll tie; execute the six-step remittance workflow with its evidence bundle and zero check; and govern the Salary Register — one monthly record, derived schedules, confidentiality enforced.</p><p><b>The second law: statutory amounts are computed by components from the accountant's current table, posted to typed custody accounts, remitted by the calendar from the reserve, and zeroed every cycle.</b> Module 3 runs the payroll itself: the Payroll Entry.</p>"),
]

